{
  "title": "Syphilis: local, systemic, and latent disease",
  "sources": [
    "CH27S16",
    "CH27S17",
    "CH27S18",
    "CH27S19",
    "CH27S22"
  ],
  "stages": [
    {
      "start": 0,
      "title": "Primary disease",
      "text": "Syphilis is caused by Treponema pallidum. In primary syphilis, focus on the local lesion: a firm, indurated, painless chancre, with firm, enlarged, nontender regional lymph nodes."
    },
    {
      "start": 11.54,
      "title": "Systemic spread",
      "text": "The supplied slides describe microorganisms draining with lymphatic fluid. Secondary syphilis has systemic manifestations, including low-grade fever, malaise, sore throat, generalized lymph-node enlargement, and skin or mucous-membrane lesions. Watch the local site connect with the wider lymphatic network."
    },
    {
      "start": 29.44,
      "title": "Latent and tertiary disease",
      "text": "Latent syphilis can be asymptomatic while medical evidence of infection remains. Absence of symptoms does not mean the infection has disappeared. Tertiary disease can include destructive gummas and neurosyphilis."
    },
    {
      "start": 43.54,
      "title": "The closest ulcer contrast",
      "text": "Keep the primary syphilitic chancre separate from chancroid. The supplied chancroid slide describes a painful, tender, soft chancre. Use the lesion findings and the disease stage together. This visual shows a schematic sequence, not a prediction for every patient."
    }
  ],
